/* Interview practice bank. Answers remain hidden until the learner reveals them. */
window.INTERVIEW_BANK=[{"id":"linux-1","topicId":"linux","concept":"Linux filesystem & mounts","tool":"Linux","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"How would you investigate a Linux server where the root filesystem is full?","answer":"First confirm which filesystem is full with df -hT and whether inode exhaustion is involved with df -i. Then identify the largest directories on that filesystem, using du with a same-filesystem option where appropriate. Check logs, application data, temporary files and deleted-but-open files before changing anything. Preserve evidence and do not remove package databases or active application state blindly.","example":"I would compare df -hT and df -i, inspect /var/log and application-specific paths, identify the owner and retention policy, then rotate or remove only approved disposable files. Afterward I would verify free space, application writes and alert thresholds.","followUp":"What would you do if df shows full but du cannot account for the space?"},{"id":"linux-2","topicId":"linux","concept":"systemd service troubleshooting","tool":"systemd · journalctl","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"A Linux service is active but users report that the application is unavailable. How do you troubleshoot it?","answer":"I would distinguish process health from application health. Check systemctl status and the effective unit, inspect recent journal entries, verify the process identity and listening socket, then call the application health endpoint locally. Check dependencies, environment variables, ports and recent deployment changes before restarting.","example":"For an Employee API I would check systemctl status employee-app, journalctl -u employee-app, ss -lntp and curl -fsS localhost:8080/health. A running Java process with a failing health endpoint is not healthy.","followUp":"Why is active (running) not a sufficient health check?"},{"id":"linux-3","topicId":"linux","concept":"Linux permissions","tool":"chmod · chown · ACL","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"An application can read a file when you test as root, but fails when started as a service user. What is your approach?","answer":"Identify the effective service identity, inspect file owner/group/mode and every parent directory. Directory execute permission is required for traversal. Correct ownership, group membership or a narrow ACL; verify access as the service account instead of testing as root.","example":"Use ps to find the process user, namei -l to inspect each path component, and sudo -u appuser test -r /path/file to prove read access. Avoid chmod 777.","followUp":"How would you grant read-only access to one service without exposing the file to every local user?"},{"id":"linux-4","topicId":"linux","concept":"process and memory analysis","tool":"ps · top · free · vmstat","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"A production VM has high CPU and memory usage. How do you identify the cause without making the incident worse?","answer":"Start with impact and time correlation. Identify top processes and process states, compare CPU, memory, swap, load average and I/O wait, inspect application metrics and recent changes. Avoid killing processes until ownership and customer impact are understood. Use a controlled mitigation and verify recovery.","example":"I would capture ps output sorted by CPU and memory, free -h, vmstat samples, service logs and request latency. Then distinguish CPU-bound work, memory leak, swap pressure or downstream wait.","followUp":"What is the difference between load average and CPU utilization?"},{"id":"linux-5","topicId":"linux","concept":"SSH and host access","tool":"SSH · sudo · PAM","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How would you secure administrative access to a fleet of Linux servers?","answer":"Use named identities, centrally managed access where possible, short-lived certificates or keys, MFA at the identity boundary, limited sudo rules and auditable sessions. Disable unnecessary direct root login and legacy password authentication after testing break-glass access. Rotate credentials and review access regularly.","example":"A deployment engineer might have a named account and sudo permission only for a controlled service restart, while automation uses a separate short-lived workload identity.","followUp":"How do you avoid locking out the team when hardening SSH?"},{"id":"shell-1","topicId":"shell","concept":"Shell quoting and argument safety","tool":"Bash · PowerShell","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Why should shell variables usually be quoted, and when would you use an array?","answer":"Unquoted variables undergo word splitting and pathname expansion, so paths with spaces or wildcard characters can become multiple unintended arguments. Quote scalar expansions. Use arrays when a command needs multiple arguments because arrays preserve argument boundaries without eval.","example":"Use args=(--region \"$REGION\" --output json) and pass them as \"${args[@]}\". Test with paths containing spaces and wildcard characters.","followUp":"What risks does eval introduce in automation?"},{"id":"shell-2","topicId":"shell","concept":"Reliable shell failure handling","tool":"Bash · exit codes","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How do you make a Bash script fail reliably when a command in a pipeline fails?","answer":"Understand each command's exit status and use explicit checks for critical operations. set -o pipefail makes a pipeline fail if a component fails, rather than only reflecting the final command's status. Use clear error messages and deliberate handling for expected non-zero outcomes.","example":"A pipeline such as curl -fsS URL | jq -e '.status == \"UP\"' should fail if curl fails or jq finds a false status, with pipefail enabled.","followUp":"Does set -e guarantee that every command failure terminates a Bash script?"},{"id":"shell-3","topicId":"shell","concept":"Idempotent automation","tool":"Bash · Python · Ansible","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What does idempotency mean in operations automation, and why does it matter?","answer":"An operation is idempotent when running it repeatedly converges to the same intended state without introducing extra side effects. It makes retries, scheduled jobs and recovery safer. The script should inspect current state and only make the required change.","example":"A directory-creation task should ensure the directory exists rather than fail or create a different directory on every run. Ansible state: present is a common pattern.","followUp":"How would you make an API call safe to retry when it creates a resource?"},{"id":"shell-4","topicId":"shell","concept":"Python vs Bash","tool":"Python · Bash","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"When would you choose Python instead of Bash for an operational tool?","answer":"Bash is effective for composing host commands and short scripts. Python is usually better for structured data, APIs, pagination, reusable modules, complex error handling and automated tests. Choose based on the task, not habit, and keep either solution observable and safe.","example":"A short log rotation wrapper may suit Bash. A cloud inventory tool that paginates APIs, handles rate limits and emits JSON is usually easier to maintain in Python.","followUp":"How would you handle retries and rate limits in a Python API client?"},{"id":"shell-5","topicId":"shell","concept":"safe file cleanup","tool":"find · xargs · rm","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How would you design a cleanup script that deletes old files without risking production data?","answer":"Validate the input path, constrain it to an allow-listed directory, quote paths, log candidate files and support dry-run. Require explicit apply mode, avoid following unsafe symlinks, and apply a retention policy approved by the data owner. Verify the result and preserve audit evidence.","example":"First run find to print candidate files and their sizes. Review the list. Only then run the exact same bounded query with deletion enabled, and test the script in a temporary directory.","followUp":"What protections would you add against someone passing / as the target directory?"},{"id":"git-1","topicId":"git","concept":"Git staging and commit model","tool":"Git index · git diff","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain the difference between a working tree, staging area and commit in Git.","answer":"The working tree contains current file edits. The index or staging area is the proposed snapshot for the next commit. A commit records that snapshot with metadata and parent history. Reviewing both unstaged and staged diffs helps prevent accidental inclusion of generated files or secrets.","example":"Use git diff to inspect unstaged changes, git add selected paths, git diff --cached to review the staged snapshot, then commit with a focused message.","followUp":"Why can git add . be risky in an infrastructure repository?"},{"id":"git-2","topicId":"git","concept":"remote and upstream tracking","tool":"GitHub · origin · push -u","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"What is origin, and what does git push -u origin feature/login do?","answer":"origin is a conventional name for a configured remote repository. The command pushes the local branch to that remote and sets its upstream tracking reference, so later push and pull commands know the default remote branch.","example":"After creating feature/login, git push -u origin feature/login publishes it and records the upstream. git branch -vv shows tracking information.","followUp":"How would you verify that you are pushing to the expected repository and branch?"},{"id":"git-3","topicId":"git","concept":"merge vs rebase","tool":"Git merge · rebase","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"When would you use merge versus rebase in a team workflow?","answer":"Merge joins histories and preserves the fact that branches diverged. Rebase replays commits onto a new base, creating new commit IDs and a linear history. Rebase is useful for local cleanup, while shared history should not be rewritten without coordination. Follow the team's branch policy.","example":"I may rebase a local feature branch on the latest main before opening a PR. For a shared integration branch, I prefer the agreed merge strategy and avoid force-pushing rewritten history.","followUp":"How does git revert differ from git reset when undoing a change?"},{"id":"git-4","topicId":"git","concept":"recover a lost commit","tool":"git reflog · git fsck","level":"ADVANCED","question":"A developer reset a branch and believes a commit is lost. How can you recover it?","answer":"First stop destructive cleanup and inspect git reflog, which records recent local reference movements. Identify the commit hash and create a recovery branch pointing to it. If reflog is unavailable, dangling objects may be discoverable with fsck. Verify the recovered content before restoring shared history.","example":"Run git reflog, then git switch -c recovery/<name> <commit-hash>. Review with git show and tests before cherry-picking or merging.","followUp":"What is the difference between reflog recovery and reverting a commit already pushed?"},{"id":"git-5","topicId":"git","concept":"secure Git workflow","tool":"branch protection · CODEOWNERS","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What controls would you put around a production branch?","answer":"Require pull requests, status checks, review by code owners, signed or verified commits where required, restricted bypass, protected tags/releases and least-privilege tokens. Add secret scanning and prevent direct pushes. Define emergency bypass with audit and post-review.","example":"A production branch could require two approvals for Terraform or workflow changes, passing tests and security scans, and release approval from a protected environment.","followUp":"How would you protect a GitHub Actions workflow from an untrusted pull request?"},{"id":"network-1","topicId":"network","concept":"DNS resolution path","tool":"DNS · dig · resolv.conf","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"A user reports that a service hostname does not resolve. How do you troubleshoot?","answer":"Check the exact hostname, resolver configuration and search domain. Query the configured resolver and an authoritative server where appropriate. Inspect record type, TTL, delegation, split-horizon policy and recent changes. Compare results from affected and unaffected networks.","example":"Use getent hosts service.internal and dig service.internal A. Compare with dig @<resolver> and inspect CNAME chains and TTL rather than assuming the application is at fault.","followUp":"Why might DNS work from a laptop but fail from a container or VPC?"},{"id":"network-2","topicId":"network","concept":"TCP timeout vs connection refused","tool":"TCP · nc · ss","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What is the difference between a connection timeout and connection refused?","answer":"Timeout usually means no successful response arrived within the timeout, often due to routing, filtering or an unresponsive destination. Refused generally means the destination or an intermediary actively rejected the connection, often because nothing is listening or a rule rejects it. Confirm with route, listener and firewall evidence.","example":"Use nc -vz host port, ss -lntp on the server, route inspection and flow/firewall logs. Test from the same network namespace as the failing client.","followUp":"What does it suggest when the TCP connection succeeds but the HTTP request fails?"},{"id":"network-3","topicId":"network","concept":"TLS handshake diagnosis","tool":"TLS · openssl · certificates","level":"ADVANCED","question":"A service works over HTTP but fails over HTTPS with a certificate error. What do you check?","answer":"Inspect certificate expiry, SAN hostname, trust chain, intermediate certificates, SNI, supported protocol/cipher and client clock. Compare the certificate actually presented by the endpoint with the expected certificate. Do not disable verification as a fix.","example":"Use openssl s_client with the correct -servername, inspect the chain and verify the hostname using the client library or curl -v.","followUp":"What is SNI and why does it matter when multiple hostnames share an IP?"},{"id":"network-4","topicId":"network","concept":"CIDR and VPC subnet design","tool":"CIDR · route tables · VPC","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How would you design subnets for a highly available three-tier AWS application?","answer":"Choose a non-overlapping VPC range with future growth. Place public load balancers and private application/data tiers across multiple availability zones. Associate deliberate route tables, control egress through NAT or endpoints and restrict east-west traffic. Reserve address capacity for scaling and managed services.","example":"For example, use separate /24 subnets for public, app and data tiers across two AZs within a /16 VPC, then document routes and security-group relationships.","followUp":"Why should database instances usually not need a default route to an internet gateway?"},{"id":"network-5","topicId":"network","concept":"HTTP load balancing","tool":"HTTP · ALB · health checks","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How would you troubleshoot intermittent 502 or 504 responses through a load balancer?","answer":"Correlate load balancer logs with target health, app logs and latency. 502 often indicates invalid or reset upstream responses; 504 indicates the upstream did not respond within timeout. Check keepalive, connection pool, backend saturation, TLS, health checks and deployment events.","example":"Compare target status, p95 latency and request IDs across load balancer and app logs. Test the target directly and review timeout values across each hop.","followUp":"How can a TCP health check report healthy while a service is returning 500?"},{"id":"build-1","topicId":"build","concept":"CI build lifecycle","tool":"Maven · Gradle · build tools","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"What is the difference between compiling, testing, packaging and verifying an application?","answer":"Compilation translates source into executable artifacts. Unit and integration tests check behaviour at different boundaries. Packaging creates a deployable artifact. Verification runs the lifecycle and checks needed before release, which may include tests, quality checks and integration steps.","example":"For Maven, mvn clean verify starts from a clean output directory and runs the configured lifecycle through verification. Inspect test reports and target artifact.","followUp":"Why is a successful compile not enough evidence for a production release?"},{"id":"build-2","topicId":"build","concept":"Dependency management","tool":"Maven · npm · lockfiles","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How do you manage dependencies and reduce supply-chain risk?","answer":"Use trusted registries, version constraints or lockfiles, review transitive dependency trees and update through tested changes. Scan for known vulnerabilities and licensing concerns. Avoid mutable versions and validate the exact dependency graph used to build the artifact.","example":"For Maven, inspect mvn dependency:tree. For Node, commit package-lock.json and use npm ci in CI so installs follow the lockfile.","followUp":"What is a transitive dependency, and how might it introduce a vulnerability?"},{"id":"build-3","topicId":"build","concept":"build reproducibility","tool":"Maven · containers · toolchains","level":"ADVANCED","question":"A build succeeds on a developer laptop but fails in CI. How do you approach it?","answer":"Compare toolchain versions, OS, environment variables, filesystem assumptions, dependency resolution and local caches. Reproduce from a clean checkout in the same container/runtime as CI. Pin the build toolchain and remove hidden local state.","example":"Pin Java 17 and Maven, run clean verify in a clean runner and retain logs showing the exact JDK and Maven versions.","followUp":"What information would you record to make the build reproducible six months later?"},{"id":"build-4","topicId":"build","concept":"artifact immutability","tool":"SHA-256 · artifact repository","level":"ADVANCED","question":"Why should a release artifact be immutable, and how do you promote it across environments?","answer":"An artifact version should identify the same bytes for its lifetime. Build once, calculate a digest, publish to an immutable version, and promote that same artifact through test and production. Record source commit and build provenance so rollback and audits are reliable.","example":"Upload employee-management-app-1.0.3.jar once, record SHA-256, deploy the exact downloaded artifact to staging and production and compare digests.","followUp":"What is the difference between an artifact checksum and signed build provenance?"},{"id":"build-5","topicId":"build","concept":"SBOM and provenance","tool":"SBOM · SLSA · attestations","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What is an SBOM, and how does it help during a vulnerability response?","answer":"A Software Bill of Materials inventories components in an artifact. It helps identify affected applications when a new vulnerability is disclosed. It should be linked to an exact artifact digest and supplemented by provenance that explains source and builder identity.","example":"Generate an SBOM for an image or JAR, store it beside the digest and query it when a CVE affects a dependency version.","followUp":"Does an SBOM prove that an artifact was built by a trusted workflow?"},{"id":"cicd-1","topicId":"cicd","concept":"CI vs CD","tool":"GitHub Actions · Jenkins","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain continuous integration, continuous delivery and continuous deployment.","answer":"Continuous integration frequently integrates small changes and runs automated validation. Continuous delivery keeps software ready to release through automated build/test/package stages, with release approval potentially manual. Continuous deployment automatically releases every change that passes the defined gates.","example":"A pull request triggers tests and scans; a merge builds an immutable artifact; an approved release job deploys it. Full automatic deployment would remove the manual production approval.","followUp":"What is the purpose of keeping CI independent from production deployment credentials?"},{"id":"cicd-2","topicId":"cicd","concept":"GitHub Actions permissions","tool":"GitHub Actions · OIDC","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Why should GitHub Actions permissions be declared explicitly?","answer":"Workflow tokens inherit permissions that can be broader than a job needs. Explicit least privilege limits damage if a step or third-party action is compromised. Job-level permissions should be granted only where needed, and deployment identity should be protected separately.","example":"A PR build needs contents: read. A trusted deploy job may require id-token: write to assume a scoped cloud role, but should not receive broad repository administration.","followUp":"Why is pull_request_target dangerous if it runs code from an untrusted pull request?"},{"id":"cicd-3","topicId":"cicd","concept":"deployment strategy","tool":"Rolling · blue/green · canary","level":"ADVANCED","question":"Compare rolling, blue/green and canary deployments. How do you choose?","answer":"Rolling replaces instances gradually and is resource-efficient but can temporarily mix versions. Blue/green maintains two environments and shifts traffic, enabling fast switchback but costs more. Canary exposes a small cohort first and uses metrics to decide promotion, but requires good routing and evaluation.","example":"For a high-risk service, route 5% to a canary, compare error rate and p95 latency against control, then progressively increase traffic or abort.","followUp":"What metrics and bake time would you require before promoting a canary?"},{"id":"cicd-4","topicId":"cicd","concept":"pipeline security","tool":"OIDC · secrets · action pinning","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you secure a pipeline that deploys to AWS?","answer":"Separate untrusted PR validation from trusted deployment. Use OIDC for short-lived credentials with tight trust conditions on repository, branch and environment. Minimize job permissions, pin third-party actions, protect environments and never expose secrets to fork-controlled code.","example":"A protected main-branch environment assumes a role that can deploy one service to one environment, while pull-request jobs cannot request the OIDC token.","followUp":"How would you investigate an unexpected AssumeRole event from GitHub Actions?"},{"id":"cicd-5","topicId":"cicd","concept":"rollback and database changes","tool":"expand/contract migration","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you design rollback when an application release includes a database migration?","answer":"Prefer backward-compatible expand/contract migrations. Add new schema first, deploy code supporting old and new forms, migrate data, then remove old fields after a safe window. Application rollback is only safe if the schema remains compatible and data changes can be reconciled.","example":"Release A adds a nullable column, Release B writes both fields, data is migrated, Release C reads the new field, and a later release drops the old field.","followUp":"What would you do if a destructive migration already ran and rollback is requested?"},{"id":"containers-1","topicId":"containers","concept":"container vs virtual machine","tool":"Docker · container runtime","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain the difference between a container and a virtual machine.","answer":"A VM virtualizes hardware and runs its own guest OS kernel. A typical Linux container isolates processes using kernel namespaces and cgroups while sharing the host kernel. Containers are usually lighter and start faster, but share kernel security boundaries and require image/runtime controls.","example":"Docker packages the app and its runtime dependencies into an image; the container process runs isolated on the host kernel. A VM may run a different guest kernel.","followUp":"What does a container image contain, and what does it not contain?"},{"id":"containers-2","topicId":"containers","concept":"Dockerfile multi-stage builds","tool":"Docker · BuildKit","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Why use a multi-stage Docker build for a Java application?","answer":"It separates compilation tooling from the runtime image. The builder stage contains JDK, Maven and source; the runtime stage copies only the packaged artifact into a smaller JRE image. This reduces attack surface and image size and keeps build tools out of production.","example":"Build with a Maven/JDK stage, then copy target/app.jar into a pinned slim Java runtime image and run as a non-root user.","followUp":"What should be excluded from the Docker build context with .dockerignore?"},{"id":"containers-3","topicId":"containers","concept":"container networking","tool":"Docker Compose · bridge network","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Why can one container not reach another at localhost?","answer":"Each container normally has its own network namespace, so localhost refers to that container. Attach services to the same user-defined network and use service DNS names. Publish only ports that need host access.","example":"In Compose, an API connects to postgres://db:5432 rather than localhost:5432; the database port need not be published publicly.","followUp":"How would you troubleshoot a container that can resolve a service name but cannot connect to its port?"},{"id":"containers-4","topicId":"containers","concept":"container hardening","tool":"non-root · capabilities · seccomp","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What controls would you apply to harden a production container?","answer":"Use trusted pinned base images, scan and verify digests, run as non-root, drop unnecessary capabilities, avoid privileged mode, use read-only root filesystem where possible, scope writable mounts, set resource limits and manage secrets outside the image.","example":"Run the app with a fixed non-root UID, read-only root filesystem and a dedicated writable temp mount; validate actual application behaviour under those restrictions.","followUp":"Why is running as non-root helpful but not sufficient by itself?"},{"id":"containers-5","topicId":"containers","concept":"image supply chain","tool":"registry · digest · signing","level":"ADVANCED","question":"Why should deployments use image digests rather than relying only on a mutable tag?","answer":"A tag can be moved to different image bytes, so the deployed content can change without a manifest change. A digest identifies exact content. Signatures and provenance add publisher and build identity; admission policy can verify trusted origin.","example":"Promote registry.example/employee-api@sha256:... from test to production and verify the signature and expected builder identity.","followUp":"What is the difference between scanning an image and verifying its provenance?"},{"id":"k8s-1","topicId":"k8s","concept":"Kubernetes architecture","tool":"API server · scheduler · controller manager","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain the main Kubernetes control-plane components and the kubelet.","answer":"The API server is the control-plane front door and stores validated objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to nodes. Controllers reconcile observed state toward declared state. Kubelet on each node manages Pods through the container runtime and reports status.","example":"A Deployment declares three replicas. The controller creates a ReplicaSet, Pods are scheduled, and kubelets start containers on selected nodes.","followUp":"What happens after you delete one Pod managed by a Deployment?"},{"id":"k8s-2","topicId":"k8s","concept":"Deployment vs StatefulSet vs DaemonSet","tool":"Kubernetes workload controllers","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"When would you choose Deployment, StatefulSet or DaemonSet?","answer":"Deployment suits stateless replicated applications with interchangeable Pods. StatefulSet provides stable identity and ordered lifecycle for stateful workloads. DaemonSet runs a Pod on each eligible node, commonly for agents such as log collectors or node monitoring.","example":"Employee API replicas use Deployment. A database operator may manage StatefulSets. A node log agent may use DaemonSet.","followUp":"Would you normally run a stateless web API as a StatefulSet? Why or why not?"},{"id":"k8s-3","topicId":"k8s","concept":"Kubernetes probes","tool":"readiness · liveness · startup","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Explain startup, readiness and liveness probes and give an example of misuse.","answer":"Startup probe allows slow initialization before other probes become active. Readiness decides whether a Pod should receive traffic. Liveness detects a process that needs restart. A liveness probe that depends on a database can restart all Pods during a database outage, amplifying the failure.","example":"A Java API might use startup to allow warmup, readiness to test whether it can serve requests and liveness to detect an internally stuck process.","followUp":"What happens to Service endpoints when a Pod becomes not ready?"},{"id":"k8s-4","topicId":"k8s","concept":"Kubernetes resources and scheduling","tool":"requests · limits · HPA","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do requests and limits affect scheduling and runtime behaviour?","answer":"Requests are used by the scheduler for placement and influence resource utilization calculations. Limits cap resource use; CPU limits can throttle and memory-limit breaches can cause OOMKilled. HPA depends on metrics and usually compares utilization with requests. Bad requests can cause overcommitment or poor scaling.","example":"Inspect kubectl describe pod, node allocatable capacity and metrics to distinguish Pending, CPU throttling and OOM events.","followUp":"Why might an HPA not scale even when CPU is high?"},{"id":"k8s-5","topicId":"k8s","concept":"Kubernetes networking and policies","tool":"Service · Ingress · NetworkPolicy","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How does traffic flow from an external client to a Kubernetes Pod?","answer":"External traffic may enter through a cloud load balancer or ingress/gateway controller. The controller routes to a Service, which selects ready Pods using labels and endpoint data. NetworkPolicy may constrain ingress/egress depending on CNI support. DNS and port mapping must also be correct.","example":"Trace a request through DNS, ingress rules, Service selector, EndpointSlices and the target Pod. If there are no endpoints, inspect selector mismatch and readiness first.","followUp":"What would you check if a Service exists but no traffic reaches its Pods?"},{"id":"k8s-6","topicId":"k8s","concept":"Kubernetes disruption management","tool":"PDB · topology spread","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do PodDisruptionBudgets and topology spread constraints improve availability?","answer":"A PDB limits voluntary evictions so enough replicas remain available during operations such as drain. Topology spread and anti-affinity distribute replicas across nodes or zones. Neither prevents every involuntary failure, and overly strict constraints can block scheduling or maintenance.","example":"For a critical API, keep several replicas across zones and a PDB that preserves the required minimum availability during planned node maintenance.","followUp":"Why can a PDB block a node drain?"},{"id":"iac-1","topicId":"iac","concept":"Terraform lifecycle","tool":"Terraform plan · apply","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain the Terraform workflow and why the plan stage matters.","answer":"Terraform configuration declares desired infrastructure. Init installs providers and configures modules/backend. Plan compares configuration, state and real infrastructure to propose changes. Apply executes a reviewed plan. State maps configuration addresses to remote objects. Plan review is essential because replacements and destroys can be disruptive.","example":"Run fmt, init, validate and plan in CI. A protected deployment workflow reviews a saved plan before apply, with state locking and backups.","followUp":"What is the risk of applying a plan generated before the state changed?"},{"id":"iac-2","topicId":"iac","concept":"Terraform state","tool":"remote backend · locking","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Why is remote state and state locking important for a team?","answer":"State is Terraform's mapping of resources to real objects and contains sensitive metadata. A remote backend provides shared access and centralized controls. Locking prevents concurrent state mutations. Encryption, access restriction, versioning and recovery are essential.","example":"Use an approved encrypted remote backend with versioned backups and locking; separate state boundaries by environment and ownership.","followUp":"What would you do if a Terraform apply is stuck while holding a state lock?"},{"id":"iac-3","topicId":"iac","concept":"Terraform modules","tool":"modules · variables · outputs","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How do you design a reusable Terraform module without making it overly generic?","answer":"Expose intentional inputs, validation, clear outputs and documented assumptions. Keep secure defaults inside the module, pin provider/module versions and avoid dozens of unrelated booleans. A module should represent a reusable architectural unit with a stable interface.","example":"A VPC module accepts CIDR, AZs and subnet sizing and exports VPC/subnet IDs. It should not silently create world-open admin access.","followUp":"How do you version a module when multiple environments consume it?"},{"id":"iac-4","topicId":"iac","concept":"Terraform drift and import","tool":"terraform import · plan","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you bring an existing manually created resource under Terraform management?","answer":"Confirm ownership and scope, write matching configuration, import the object into the correct resource address, then run plan and reconcile until no unintended changes remain. Import changes state binding, not the desired configuration itself.","example":"Import a test VPC into a dedicated state, write its CIDR and tags, inspect the refreshed plan and avoid apply until replacement/destruction is ruled out.","followUp":"What can happen if one cloud object is imported into two Terraform resource addresses?"},{"id":"iac-5","topicId":"iac","concept":"Terraform security review","tool":"IAM · policy as code","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What do you inspect in a Terraform pull request before approving it?","answer":"Review destructive actions, public exposure, IAM permissions, secrets, encryption, logging, network paths, data lifecycle and cost. Validate formatting and syntax, run policy/security scans and review the plan from a trusted context. Treat valid syntax as different from safe design.","example":"Flag an S3 policy that enables public access or an ingress rule from 0.0.0.0/0 even if terraform validate succeeds.","followUp":"How would you prevent public storage and overly broad IAM from reaching production?"},{"id":"cloud-1","topicId":"cloud","concept":"AWS shared responsibility","tool":"AWS security model","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain the AWS shared responsibility model with an example.","answer":"AWS is responsible for security of the cloud, including facilities, hardware and managed infrastructure. Customers are responsible for security in the cloud, such as identity, data, access configuration, workload patching depending on service model, network controls and encryption choices. Responsibility varies by service.","example":"For EC2, the customer manages guest OS patching and instance access. For a managed database, AWS manages more of the underlying platform, but the customer still manages data access, schema and identity.","followUp":"How does the responsibility boundary differ between EC2 and a managed database?"},{"id":"cloud-2","topicId":"cloud","concept":"IAM roles and OIDC","tool":"IAM · STS · GitHub Actions","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How can GitHub Actions access AWS without storing long-lived access keys?","answer":"Configure an OIDC identity provider and a role trust policy that checks the expected issuer, audience and repository/branch/environment subject. The workflow requests a short-lived token, assumes the role and receives temporary credentials scoped by permission policies.","example":"A protected main branch can assume a deployment role for one application. Pull-request workflows receive no production role and only have read-only repository permission.","followUp":"What is the difference between the role trust policy and its permission policy?"},{"id":"cloud-3","topicId":"cloud","concept":"VPC public vs private subnet","tool":"VPC · IGW · NAT · endpoints","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What makes an AWS subnet public or private?","answer":"A subnet is generally considered public when its route table has a route to an internet gateway, and the resource also needs public addressing and security rules to be internet reachable. Private subnets have no direct IGW route; outbound connectivity may use NAT or VPC endpoints.","example":"Place the internet-facing ALB in public subnets and application/database resources in private subnets. Use endpoints for supported AWS service traffic when justified.","followUp":"Does adding an internet-gateway route alone make a private EC2 instance reachable from the internet?"},{"id":"cloud-4","topicId":"cloud","concept":"AWS high availability","tool":"Multi-AZ · ALB · Auto Scaling","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How would you make a web application highly available in AWS?","answer":"Distribute load balancer targets across multiple Availability Zones, keep application instances stateless or externalize state, use health-based routing and autoscaling, and ensure data tier backups/replication match recovery objectives. Monitor quotas, capacity and dependency failures.","example":"An ALB spans two public subnets and routes to private application targets in two AZs. Deployments keep healthy capacity while new targets pass application checks.","followUp":"What failure modes remain even after deploying across two Availability Zones?"},{"id":"cloud-5","topicId":"cloud","concept":"AWS cost controls","tool":"Budgets · Cost Explorer · tagging","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you control unexpected AWS costs in a learning or production account?","answer":"Use budgets and anomaly alerts, cost allocation tags, least privilege, quotas and expiry ownership. Review high drivers such as NAT, load balancers, public IPv4, snapshots, data transfer and log retention. Alerts do not always stop spend, so containment and cleanup processes matter.","example":"Tag labs with owner, project and expiry. Review billing by service and region and verify deletion of resources such as load balancers, volumes, IPs and snapshots.","followUp":"Which common networking resources can generate charges even when application traffic is low?"},{"id":"gitops-1","topicId":"gitops","concept":"GitOps principles","tool":"Argo CD · Flux","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What is GitOps, and how is it different from a push-based deployment pipeline?","answer":"GitOps stores desired configuration in Git and uses a controller to continuously compare and reconcile live state with declared state. A push pipeline issues changes at deployment time; GitOps adds ongoing drift detection, reconciliation and auditable desired-state history.","example":"A protected Git change updates an image digest. Argo CD detects the revision, renders manifests, syncs them and evaluates application health.","followUp":"What happens when someone manually changes a field that GitOps owns?"},{"id":"gitops-2","topicId":"gitops","concept":"Helm vs Kustomize","tool":"Helm · Kustomize","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"When would you choose Helm over Kustomize, or vice versa?","answer":"Helm packages configurable templates into versioned charts and supports release lifecycle. Kustomize overlays patches on a base without requiring a template language. The choice depends on packaging, reuse, ecosystem and complexity. Always inspect rendered manifests.","example":"Use Helm for a reusable application chart with values, or Kustomize overlays for environment-specific changes to a common manifest base.","followUp":"Can a manifest be valid YAML but still be unsafe to deploy? Give an example."},{"id":"gitops-3","topicId":"gitops","concept":"GitOps drift and prune","tool":"Argo CD · reconciliation","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What are the risks of automated sync, self-heal and prune?","answer":"Automated sync applies source changes. Self-heal reverts manual drift. Prune deletes resources no longer declared in source. These features need clear ownership and protections for critical resources, because accidental manifest deletion can remove live infrastructure.","example":"Keep automated sync for a controlled app but review prune carefully for persistent data and shared resources. Use policies and protected resource annotations where supported.","followUp":"How would you recover from an accidental prune?"},{"id":"gitops-4","topicId":"gitops","concept":"GitOps promotion","tool":"image digest · overlays","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you promote the same artifact from development to production with GitOps?","answer":"Build once and identify the image by immutable digest. Promote that digest through reviewed environment overlays or release metadata. Each environment has explicit policy, approvals and health gates. Avoid rebuilding different bytes per environment.","example":"A dev overlay uses employee-api@sha256:abc. After tests, a PR updates staging and then production to the same digest.","followUp":"How do you roll back a bad GitOps release and preserve the audit trail?"},{"id":"gitops-5","topicId":"gitops","concept":"GitOps security","tool":"RBAC · secrets · admission","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you secure a GitOps controller and its repository credentials?","answer":"Limit controller scope to specific namespaces and resources, use least-privilege repository credentials, prefer external secret references or encrypted secret workflows, protect branches and verify signed images through admission controls. Audit sync operations and isolate tenants.","example":"An Argo CD instance for a team is restricted to that team's namespaces and cannot modify cluster-wide RBAC or unrelated secrets.","followUp":"Why should a GitOps controller not have cluster-admin rights by default?"},{"id":"observability-1","topicId":"observability","concept":"Metrics, logs and traces","tool":"Prometheus · Loki · OpenTelemetry","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"Explain the differences between metrics, logs and distributed traces.","answer":"Metrics are numeric time-series useful for trends and alerts. Logs are timestamped event records with context. Traces connect spans across services to show one request's path and latency. They complement one another and should share correlation metadata without creating high-cardinality metric labels.","example":"An alert sees rising p95 latency in Prometheus, the trace shows a slow database span and structured logs identify a timeout for the same trace ID.","followUp":"Why should request IDs usually be logged or traced rather than used as metric labels?"},{"id":"observability-2","topicId":"observability","concept":"RED and USE methods","tool":"latency · traffic · errors · saturation","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How would you design a dashboard for a service running in production?","answer":"Use RED for request-driven services: rate, errors and duration/latency. USE helps infrastructure resources: utilization, saturation and errors. Add deployment annotations, dependency health, SLO budget and links to runbooks. Avoid dashboards that only show host CPU.","example":"For an API, show request rate, 5xx ratio, p50/p95/p99 latency, queue depth, database pool saturation and release version.","followUp":"How would you detect a slow dependency before it causes an outage?"},{"id":"observability-3","topicId":"observability","concept":"SLO and error budget","tool":"SLI · SLO · burn rate","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What is an error budget and how do teams use it?","answer":"An SLO defines a reliability target over a window using an SLI. The error budget is the allowed unreliability implied by that target. Burn-rate alerts show how quickly the budget is consumed and can guide release freezes, mitigation and reliability investment.","example":"For a 99.9% availability target, the budget is 0.1% of eligible requests in the defined window. A fast burn can pause risky releases while the team mitigates.","followUp":"Why are multi-window burn-rate alerts often better than a single threshold?"},{"id":"observability-4","topicId":"observability","concept":"alert quality","tool":"Prometheus · Alertmanager","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What makes an operational alert actionable rather than noisy?","answer":"It should indicate user impact or a meaningful risk, have an owner, severity, signal, threshold and runbook. Group related symptoms, inhibit duplicates, tune for a useful window and test routing. Alerts should lead to an action rather than simply describe a metric fluctuation.","example":"Page on sustained elevated 5xx rate or SLO burn, not on every brief CPU spike. Include service, environment, dashboard and runbook links.","followUp":"What would you do with an alert that pages frequently but rarely requires action?"},{"id":"observability-5","topicId":"observability","concept":"observability cost and privacy","tool":"OpenTelemetry · retention","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you control telemetry cost without losing incident evidence?","answer":"Set sampling and retention based on service criticality and diagnostic needs. Use bounded metric labels, avoid duplicate logs, redact sensitive fields and use tiered storage. Preserve high-value audit/security events under their required retention policy.","example":"Keep request IDs in traces/logs, sample routine successful traces, retain errors and high-latency traces at higher rates and expire verbose debug logs sooner.","followUp":"What personal or secret data should never be emitted into application logs?"},{"id":"security-1","topicId":"security","concept":"DevSecOps shift left","tool":"SAST · DAST · SCA","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"What does shift-left security mean, and what are its limits?","answer":"It moves useful security feedback earlier into design, code review and CI so defects are cheaper to fix. SAST, dependency scanning, secret scanning and IaC checks are helpful but do not replace runtime testing, threat modelling, secure operations or human review.","example":"A PR scans dependencies and Terraform before merge, while DAST and runtime controls validate the deployed application later.","followUp":"Why should a green security scanner not be treated as proof that an application is secure?"},{"id":"security-2","topicId":"security","concept":"secrets management","tool":"Secrets Manager · Vault · OIDC","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How do you prevent secrets from leaking through Git and CI pipelines?","answer":"Do not store secrets in source, images or logs. Use short-lived federated credentials where possible, secret managers for runtime secrets, least-privilege access, redaction, rotation and audit. Treat forks, artifacts and debug output as sensitive surfaces.","example":"GitHub Actions can assume AWS through OIDC rather than storing access keys. The application retrieves a database secret at runtime from an approved secret store.","followUp":"What steps do you take after discovering a secret committed to a public repository?"},{"id":"security-3","topicId":"security","concept":"SAST vs DAST vs SCA","tool":"security testing","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Compare SAST, DAST and SCA, and describe where each fits in a pipeline.","answer":"SAST analyzes source or bytecode for coding weaknesses. DAST tests a running application from the outside. SCA inventories dependencies and checks known vulnerabilities and licenses. They see different risks and should be combined with IaC and image scanning where relevant.","example":"Run SAST and SCA on pull requests, scan the final image before publish, and run DAST against a test deployment with safe test data.","followUp":"How do you prioritize a high-severity dependency finding that is not reachable in your deployed path?"},{"id":"security-4","topicId":"security","concept":"software supply-chain trust","tool":"SBOM · signatures · provenance","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you verify that a container image came from an approved build pipeline?","answer":"Identify the immutable image digest, verify a signature, validate signer identity and issuer claims, inspect provenance and SBOM, and enforce admission policy. Trust must be bound to expected repository and workflow context, not just any valid signature.","example":"The cluster admission policy accepts images signed by a protected repository workflow with the expected OIDC issuer and rejects unrelated publishers.","followUp":"Why is a signature from an unknown builder insufficient?"},{"id":"security-5","topicId":"security","concept":"threat modelling","tool":"STRIDE · trust boundaries","level":"ADVANCED","question":"Walk me through how you would threat-model a CI/CD deployment path.","answer":"Identify assets, actors, entry points and trust boundaries from source control through runner, identity token, cloud role, registry and deployment target. Enumerate threats such as spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and privilege escalation. Assign mitigations and verification.","example":"For GitHub-to-AWS OIDC, restrict the subject claim, job permissions, role permissions and deployment environment; audit assumptions and artifact provenance.","followUp":"What is one threat that a network firewall alone would not prevent in this architecture?"},{"id":"sre-1","topicId":"sre","concept":"SRE vs DevOps","tool":"SRE · reliability engineering","level":"FOUNDATION","question":"How do you explain the relationship between DevOps and SRE?","answer":"DevOps is a cultural and engineering approach that improves collaboration and delivery flow. SRE applies software engineering and operational practices to reliability, often using SLOs, error budgets, automation and incident management. They overlap and both aim for reliable, sustainable delivery.","example":"A DevOps team builds delivery automation and shared ownership; SRE practices define service objectives, error-budget policy and reliability improvements.","followUp":"How can an error budget influence release decisions?"},{"id":"sre-2","topicId":"sre","concept":"incident response","tool":"severity · incident command","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"You are on call and a production service is failing. What do you do first?","answer":"Confirm impact and scope, declare severity, establish an incident channel and assign roles. Preserve evidence, inspect recent changes and key signals, communicate known facts and hypotheses separately, choose a reversible mitigation and verify recovery. Keep a timeline and follow up with a blameless review.","example":"If error rate spikes after a release, pause further changes, compare the last known-good version, test rollback and verify customer-facing synthetic transactions while keeping stakeholders updated.","followUp":"Why is restarting everything immediately often a poor first response?"},{"id":"sre-3","topicId":"sre","concept":"cascading failures","tool":"timeouts · retries · circuit breakers","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How can retries make a dependency outage worse, and how do you prevent it?","answer":"Retries multiply traffic when a dependency is already overloaded. Use bounded retries with backoff and jitter only for safe operations, strict end-to-end timeouts, circuit breakers, bulkheads and load shedding. Use idempotency for operations with side effects.","example":"A payment request should carry an idempotency key; a client should not blindly repeat a charge after an ambiguous timeout. A breaker can stop calls to an unhealthy dependency.","followUp":"How do you choose retry count and timeout budget across multiple service hops?"},{"id":"sre-4","topicId":"sre","concept":"disaster recovery","tool":"RTO · RPO · backup restore","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you prove that a backup and disaster-recovery plan actually works?","answer":"Define RTO and RPO with business owners, protect backups and encryption keys, isolate copies, document dependencies and regularly restore into a separate environment. Measure actual recovery time and data loss, validate application correctness and update the plan.","example":"Restore a database snapshot into isolation, verify row counts and critical business flows, measure elapsed time and compare against RTO/RPO objectives.","followUp":"Why is a successful backup job not enough to prove recoverability?"},{"id":"sre-5","topicId":"sre","concept":"postmortem culture","tool":"blameless review · corrective actions","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What should a good incident postmortem contain?","answer":"Describe impact and timeline, detection, contributing conditions, what worked and failed, mitigation, root causes and follow-up actions with owners and due dates. Focus on system conditions rather than individual blame. Verify that actions reduce recurrence or improve detection.","example":"A postmortem could identify missing database saturation alerts, assign an owner and date, then test the new alert in a game day.","followUp":"How do you prevent postmortem action items from becoming an unowned document?"},{"id":"platform-1","topicId":"platform","concept":"platform engineering","tool":"golden paths · developer experience","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What is platform engineering, and what problem does an internal developer platform solve?","answer":"Platform engineering builds reusable capabilities and self-service workflows that help product teams deliver safely with less cognitive load. A platform should provide reliable interfaces and paved paths, not just centralize tickets or force teams into a portal.","example":"A Java service template could create a repository, CI pipeline, container defaults, deployment scaffold, service catalog entry and dashboard with secure defaults.","followUp":"How would you measure whether a platform is genuinely helping developers?"},{"id":"platform-2","topicId":"platform","concept":"golden path design","tool":"Backstage · templates","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What should a production-ready golden path include?","answer":"It should cover repository structure, build/test, security controls, deployment, identity, observability, ownership, runbooks and lifecycle upgrades. Provide safe defaults and an exception path for valid needs. Treat templates as maintained products.","example":"A new service template asks for service name and owner, provisions a repository and attaches a reviewed Java pipeline, Dockerfile, deployment manifest and catalog metadata.","followUp":"How do you roll out breaking changes to a template used by many teams?"},{"id":"platform-3","topicId":"platform","concept":"service catalog","tool":"Backstage · ownership metadata","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"Why is a service catalog useful during an incident?","answer":"It provides authoritative ownership, dependencies, criticality, support channel, runbook, repository, SLO and operational metadata. It reduces time spent discovering who owns a failing component. Data must be kept fresh and tied to service lifecycle.","example":"An alert links to the service catalog entry so the on-call engineer sees the owning team, dependency map, dashboard and runbook immediately.","followUp":"How would you detect stale ownership metadata?"},{"id":"platform-4","topicId":"platform","concept":"platform multi-tenancy","tool":"RBAC · quotas · isolation","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you design a multi-tenant platform that is safe for multiple application teams?","answer":"Enforce identity and resource isolation beyond the UI: scoped accounts/projects or namespaces, RBAC, network policy, quota, secret boundaries and audit. Define platform SLOs and ownership. High-sensitivity workloads may need stronger isolation than namespaces alone.","example":"A team namespace has its own service account and quotas, and cluster-wide operations are controlled by the platform team through reviewed workflows.","followUp":"Why is a namespace alone not always a sufficient security boundary?"},{"id":"platform-5","topicId":"platform","concept":"platform SLOs","tool":"platform reliability · adoption","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What metrics would you use to evaluate an internal developer platform?","answer":"Measure platform availability, provisioning lead time, template success rate, change failure rate, time to first deployment, adoption, developer satisfaction and support load. Pair efficiency metrics with security and reliability outcomes.","example":"Track how long it takes a new service to reach its first healthy deployment, and what fraction of generated services meet security/observability policy.","followUp":"How do you avoid optimizing deployment speed at the expense of reliability?"},{"id":"aiops-1","topicId":"aiops","concept":"MLOps lifecycle","tool":"model registry · drift","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What is MLOps, and how does it extend traditional DevOps?","answer":"MLOps applies reproducible build, deployment, monitoring and governance practices to ML systems while also managing data, features, training, evaluation and model versions. Data and model drift require ongoing monitoring and controlled retraining.","example":"An anomaly model is trained from versioned incident data, evaluated on a held-out time-aware test set, registered and shadowed before alert routing is enabled.","followUp":"Why is random train/test splitting risky for time-series incident data?"},{"id":"aiops-2","topicId":"aiops","concept":"RAG architecture","tool":"embeddings · vector search · retrieval","level":"ADVANCED","question":"Explain a retrieval-augmented generation pipeline and its main failure points.","answer":"RAG ingests approved sources, chunks and tags documents, creates embeddings, retrieves relevant chunks with access filters, optionally reranks and supplies evidence to the model. Failures can occur in freshness, chunking, retrieval recall, permissions, citation mapping or generation.","example":"An incident assistant retrieves the approved runbook version for the correct service, applies team access filters and cites the document section in its response.","followUp":"Why is vector similarity not a substitute for document authorization?"},{"id":"aiops-3","topicId":"aiops","concept":"LLM evaluation","tool":"prompt versioning · eval datasets","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you evaluate an LLM feature before releasing a prompt or model change?","answer":"Create a representative evaluation set with expected facts, citations, abstention behaviour and safety edge cases. Version prompt/model/retrieval settings, compare quality and latency/cost, run red-team tests and promote gradually with rollback available.","example":"A runbook summarizer is tested against known incidents and must not invent commands, must cite source sections and must abstain when evidence is missing.","followUp":"How would you detect a model regression when answers sound plausible but contain unsupported facts?"},{"id":"aiops-4","topicId":"aiops","concept":"AI agent tool safety","tool":"tool schemas · approval gates","level":"ADVANCED","question":"How do you prevent an AI agent from making unauthorized infrastructure changes?","answer":"Authorization must be enforced outside the model. Expose narrow typed tools, default to read-only, validate arguments, restrict identities and place mutations behind authenticated human approval. Bound steps and timeouts, audit calls and treat retrieved/tool text as untrusted.","example":"An incident assistant can read metrics and approved runbooks but cannot run shell commands or apply Terraform. It can propose a plan for a human to review.","followUp":"Why is a prompt saying 'do not change production' not a security control?"},{"id":"aiops-5","topicId":"aiops","concept":"LLMOps cost and observability","tool":"tokens · latency · monitoring","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What would you monitor for a production LLM-powered DevOps assistant?","answer":"Track task success and groundedness, citation correctness, refusal/safety results, latency, availability, token usage, cost by tenant and model, retrieval quality and tool errors. Use budgets and rate limits, and monitor model/prompt version changes.","example":"A dashboard tracks p95 response time, tokens per request, retrieval hit rate, human override rate and unsupported-claim evaluation score.","followUp":"How would you reduce inference cost without silently degrading answer quality?"},{"id":"finops-1","topicId":"finops","concept":"FinOps operating model","tool":"FinOps · cost allocation","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"What is FinOps and how does it work with engineering teams?","answer":"FinOps is a collaborative practice that brings finance, product and engineering together to make informed cloud-spend decisions. It emphasizes visibility, allocation, optimization and shared accountability rather than cost reduction at any expense.","example":"Tag resources by owner/project/environment, report cost per service or transaction and let teams evaluate optimization against SLO and product value.","followUp":"How do you allocate shared platform costs fairly between teams?"},{"id":"finops-2","topicId":"finops","concept":"cloud cost optimization","tool":"rightsizing · autoscaling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"How would you reduce cloud costs without damaging reliability?","answer":"Start with cost attribution and usage evidence. Look for idle or oversized resources, schedule non-production, tune autoscaling, optimize storage lifecycle and evaluate network/data-transfer drivers. Preserve backups, redundancy and security requirements. Measure savings against performance and reliability.","example":"Right-size a dev instance based on utilization and schedule it off after hours, but keep production multi-AZ and validate latency and error rates after each change.","followUp":"Which cost optimization actions are high risk and require business approval?"},{"id":"finops-3","topicId":"finops","concept":"AWS networking cost","tool":"NAT · IPv4 · data transfer","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What network-related AWS costs would you inspect in a cost review?","answer":"Review NAT gateway hours and data processing, public IPv4 address charges, load balancers, cross-AZ and internet data transfer, VPC endpoints and logging. Use flow and usage evidence to identify whether private endpoints or architecture changes are beneficial; include endpoint charges in the comparison.","example":"A workload accessing S3 heavily through NAT may benefit from a gateway endpoint, depending on architecture and regional pricing. Compare full data-path costs rather than assuming it is free.","followUp":"Why might adding VPC endpoints increase some fixed costs even while reducing NAT processing?"},{"id":"finops-4","topicId":"finops","concept":"unit economics","tool":"cost per request · cost per build","level":"ADVANCED","question":"What is unit economics in cloud operations, and why is total monthly spend alone insufficient?","answer":"Unit economics relates spend to meaningful product output such as cost per transaction, customer, request or build. Total spend can rise because usage grows; unit cost shows whether efficiency improves. Use consistent allocation and compare alongside reliability and quality.","example":"If monthly compute spend grows 20% but requests grow 50%, cost per request may improve. Validate that latency and error rate have not worsened.","followUp":"How do you allocate shared infrastructure cost into a per-service unit cost?"},{"id":"finops-5","topicId":"finops","concept":"budget anomaly response","tool":"Budgets · alerts · tagging","level":"INTERMEDIATE","question":"An AWS cost anomaly alert fires unexpectedly. What do you do?","answer":"Identify account, service, region, usage type and affected resources. Compare against normal baseline and recent changes, notify owners, contain the source safely and verify charges stop. Do not delete resources before understanding dependencies or retaining required data.","example":"Use Cost Explorer or billing data to identify an unexpected NAT or EC2 cost, map resource tags to the owner, inspect usage and then remove or resize only after approval.","followUp":"Why are budget alerts not the same as hard spending limits?"}];
